  pm-ydshl9,related,"2026-08-07T11:19:55.144Z","harness:claude-code","cli:update:dep",detected
body: "## Reproduction\n\nMeasured 2026-08-07 on the live workspace.\n\n```\npm list --status open --limit 3 --output-include id,title --json\n  -> rows carry exactly {id, title}                       # field projection, correct\n\npm get pm-n7rr --output-include title --json\n  -> item carries all 23 fields, 2,219 estimated tokens   # silently ignored\n  -> omission_receipt.has_omissions = false\n\npm get pm-n7rr --output-include item --json\n  -> children, claim_state and linked dropped; 1,846 tokens  # section filter, works\n\npm get pm-x --output-include body,acceptance_criteria,children --json\n  -> the entire `item` entity is dropped from the response\n  -> omission_receipt.has_omissions = false\n```\n\n## The defect\n\n`--output-include` is documented once, as \"Retain comma-separated fields or sections in read output\", and implemented as two different things.\n\nOn a collection read the argument names **row fields** and projection works. On an entity read the argument names **top-level sections**, so a field name matches nothing, is discarded without a diagnostic, and the caller pays the full entity cost believing it asked for less. Worse, the two namespaces are not disjoint: pass one recognised section name alongside field names and the section filter engages, dropping every unlisted section — including `item` itself, the only section the caller wanted.\n\nBoth failures are silent in the same way and in the same place. The omission receipt exists precisely to name what a bounded read withheld and which flag restores it. It reports `has_omissions: false` for a request that was ignored in full, and for a request that discarded the entity.\n\n## Why this matters more than its size suggests\n\n`pm get` is the highest-frequency read in the documented agent loop — the verification recipe in the operating rules names it first, and every claim about tracked state is supposed to be backed by it. It is also the one read with no working field projection, so the cheapest possible question, \"what is this item's title and status\", costs the same as the most expensive one. At a thousand concurrent agents that difference is the difference between a bounded and an unbounded fleet budget.\n\nThe rule this violates is already written down: a value that a surface accepts and a consumer ignores is a removal that reports success. Here the accepting surface and the blind consumer are the same command.\n\n## Boundary\n\nThe fix is not to make field names work on entity reads by accident. It is to decide, once, whether `--output-include` addresses fields or sections, to give the other concept its own declared spelling, and to make an unmatched token a refusal that teaches rather than a silent discard. Whatever is decided, the omission receipt must report it."
